<!-- ============================================================
     Chapter: Debugging Embedded Programs  (Day 7x)
     50-minute standalone x-hour — no pre-class reading required.
     Lesson plan: plans/day7x.md · Old deck: Day07x-Debugging.pptx
     ============================================================ -->

<chapter xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude" xml:id="ch-debugging">
    <title>Debugging Embedded Programs</title>
    <introduction>
        <p>
            The goal of this chapter is to give you a systematic way to find the
            bugs in your own programs.  Every program you write for the rest of
            this course, and most programs you will write after it, will be wrong
            the first time you run it; that is the normal state of programming,
            and finding the fault quickly is a skill you can learn like any
            other.
        </p>
        <p>
            This chapter covers one class day, the Wednesday x-hour, and it has no
            pre-class reading: everything we use is on these pages.  In class
            we'll first sharpen two tools you already own, the compiler's error
            messages and the <c>printf</c> statement, and then we'll add a new
            one: the STM32CubeIDE <term>interactive debugger</term>, which lets us
            pause a running program and look inside any register or variable on
            the chip.  We'll use all three on Lab 4 in the rest of the hour.
            The <xref ref="sec-debug-reference" text="title-element"/> section at
            the end collects the checklists and reference tables to come back to
            all term.
        </p>
        <objectives>
            <ul>
                <li>Recognize the common sources of C bugs, and name the practice
                    that would have prevented one of your own.</li>
                <li>Read compiler error output strategically: start at the top,
                    and read <em>up</em> from the reported line.</li>
                <li>Place a diagnostic <c>printf</c> that answers a specific
                    question, printing the expected value beside the actual one.</li>
                <li>Launch a debug session, single-step, and watch a peripheral
                    register change in the SFRs (Special Function Registers)
                    view.</li>
                <li>Set breakpoints, resume between them, and observe a variable
                    at the moment it changes.</li>
            </ul>
        </objectives>
    </introduction>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-debug-inclass">
        <title>Day 7x In-Class: Debugging Tips and Tools</title>

        <introduction>
            <!--
            INSTRUCTOR (settling, 3 min):
            Settling slide asks students to open their old Blinky project and
            click build NOW, so the project-reopen tax runs during Parts 1-2
            instead of at the start of Part 3.  Repeat it verbally once.
            -->
            <p>
                Today we'll practice finding bugs: first in a program that will
                not build, then in one that builds and runs but goes quiet, and
                then with the debugger on your own Blinky.
            </p>
            <p>
                As you sit down: find your old <c>Blinky</c> project in the
                STM32CubeIDE (the very first project of the term), open it, and
                click build and upload to your Nucleo.  Make sure it still runs.
                We'll need it when we get to the debugger.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <!-- ── Part 1 ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-debug-part1">
            <title>Part 1: Where Do Bugs Come From?</title>

            <!--
            INSTRUCTOR (6 min): show of hands over the list — raise your own
            hand throughout.  Then the 60-second write-and-share.
            -->
            <p>
                Bugs in C programs come from a short list of slips, and the same
                few account for most of the time we will spend debugging in this
                course:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>Missing or extra semicolon, <c>)</c>, or <c>}</c></li>
                <li>Incorrect register name</li>
                <li>Missing <c>#include</c> files</li>
                <li>Incorrect constant (<c>#define</c>) values</li>
                <li>Undeclared or uninitialized variables</li>
                <li>Misspelled names</li>
                <li>Type mismatches</li>
                <li>No <c>return</c> statement in a function</li>
                <li><c>=</c> or <c>==</c>?  <c>&amp;</c> or <c>&amp;&amp;</c>?
                    <c>|</c> or <c>||</c>?  <c>~</c> or <c>!</c>?</li>
                <li>Alas, many others<ellipsis/></li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Some of these will be caught by the compiler.  The ones that are
                not caught are the dangerous ones: they compile cleanly and then
                run incorrectly.
            </p>
            <p>
                A few habits prevent a large part of that list before it happens:
                plan the design on paper before typing; write in small chunks and
                test each one before moving on; give every function one clear job;
                use named constants instead of magic numbers; and keep the code
                readable, with descriptive names.  The full checklist, with the
                reasoning behind each item, is in
                <xref ref="subsec-debug-prevention"/>.
            </p>

            <activity xml:id="act-debug-bugs">
                <title>Whose Bugs Are These?</title>
                <task xml:id="task-debug-bugs-count">
                    <p>
                        Raise your hand for each of these errors you have made.
                        The instructor's hand will be up for essentially every
                        item.
                    </p>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-debug-bugs-practice">
                    <p>
                        Pick one bug for which you raised your hand.  Think about
                        which habit from the paragraph above would have caught it
                        before it cost you time.
                    </p>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <slide xml:id="sl-debug-bug-list">
                <p>How many of these have you personally made?  Raise your hand for
                   each of these errors you have made:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Missing or extra semicolon, <c>)</c>, or <c>}</c></li>
                    <li>Incorrect register name · misspelled names</li>
                    <li>Missing <c>#include</c> · wrong <c>#define</c> value</li>
                    <li>Undeclared or uninitialized variables · type mismatches</li>
                    <li>No <c>return</c> statement in a function</li>
                    <li><c>=</c> or <c>==</c>?  <c>&amp;</c> or <c>&amp;&amp;</c>?  <c>|</c> or <c>||</c>?  <c>~</c> or <c>!</c>?</li>
                    <li>Alas, many others<ellipsis/></li>
                </ul>
                <note>Your own hand stays up for essentially every item.  The compiler catches some of these; the rest compile cleanly and run wrong.</note>
            </slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-debug-practices" room="yes">
                <p>Habits that prevent most of that list.  Pick one bug for which you raised your hand, and <em>think about which habit would have caught it</em>:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Plan the design on paper first: block diagram, pseudocode, state diagram.</li>
                    <li>Write in small chunks; test each before moving on.</li>
                    <li>One clear job per function: <q>set up the UART</q>, <q>get a value from the ADC</q>.</li>
                    <li>Named constants, not magic numbers.</li>
                    <li>Readable code with descriptive names, and comments as you go.</li>
                </ul>
            </slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- ── Part 2a ────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-debug-part2a">
            <title>Part 2a: What the Compiler Is Trying to Tell You</title>

            <!--
            INSTRUCTOR (4 min): predict-then-reveal.  Students commit to a line
            number BEFORE the reveal.  The transcript is real output from the
            course toolchain (arm-none-eabi-gcc 13.3) on the listing shown.
            -->
            <p>
                Compiler error messages are the cheapest debugging tool we have:
                the compiler has already found the problem, but the line it reports
                is not always the line the mistake is on.  Here is a classmate's
                <c>main.c</c> that will not
                build.  It is Blinky, and exactly one thing is wrong with it:
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#include "stm32c0xx.h"          // all register definitions live here
#define GPIOAEN  (1U << 0)      // RCC->IOPENR bit 0: clock for GPIOA
#define LED_PIN  (1U << 5)      // GPIOA bit 5: the on-board LED

int main(void) {
    RCC->IOPENR  |= GPIOAEN      // Step 1: enable GPIOA clock
    GPIOA->MODER |= (1U << 10);  // Step 2a: set   bit 10
    GPIOA->MODER &= ~(1U << 11); // Step 2b: clear bit 11 -> PA5 output

    while (1) {
        GPIOA->ODR |= LED_PIN;   // Step 3: bit 5 = 1 -> LED on
        for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++);
        GPIOA->ODR &= ~LED_PIN;  // Step 4: bit 5 = 0 -> LED off
        for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++);
    }
    return 0;                    // never reached
}
]]></code></program>
            <p>
                The compiler reports the following.  A quick anatomy note for
                a first read of GCC output: each block starts with
                <c>file:line:column:</c>, then <c>error:</c> (what stopped the
                compiler) or <c>note:</c> (context it is adding, pointing at a
                second, related location), then the offending source line with
                a caret marking the exact column.
            </p>
            <program><code><![CDATA[
main.c: In function 'main':
main.c:2:18: error: called object is not a function or function pointer
    2 | #define GPIOAEN  (1U << 0)      // RCC->IOPENR bit 0: clock for GPIOA
      |                  ^
main.c:6:21: note: in expansion of macro 'GPIOAEN'
    6 |     RCC->IOPENR  |= GPIOAEN      // Step 1: enable GPIOA clock
      |                     ^~~~~~~
]]></code></program>

            <activity xml:id="act-debug-root-error">
                <title>Find the Real Bug</title>
                <task xml:id="task-debug-root-line2">
                    <p>
                        The first message points at line 2.  Read line 2 of the
                        listing.  Is anything wrong with it?
                    </p>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-debug-root-find">
                    <p>
                        Decide which line the actual bug is on, and what the fix
                        would be.  Commit to an answer before reading on.
                    </p>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <!-- Reveal (after the activity) -->
            <p>
                Line 2 is perfectly healthy.  The real
                bug is the missing semicolon at the end of line 6.  Both
                <c>GPIOAEN</c> and <c>GPIOA</c> are macros that expand to
                parenthesized expressions.  With the semicolon gone, the
                compiler sees the value <c>(1U &lt;&lt; 0)</c> followed
                immediately by a parenthesized expression, <c>GPIOA</c>'s own
                expansion, and in C, a value followed by a parenthesized list
                is a <em>function call</em>.  So it objects to the thing being
                <q>called</q>: the value of <c>GPIOAEN</c>, defined on line 2.
                (Had <c>GPIOA</c> been an ordinary variable, the same missing
                semicolon would have produced a plain <q>expected ';'</q>.  The
                message is baffling only because the peripheral name is itself
                a parenthesized macro.)  The line where an error is
                <em>reported</em> is often just where the compiler finally gave
                up making sense of the code.
            </p>
            <p>
                Which direction should you look?  Usually the mistake sits at or
                <em>above</em> the reported line, but this example is exactly
                the case where that fails, and it is worth seeing why.  Line 2 is
                a <c>#define</c>, and a <c>#define</c> is not code that runs
                where it is written; it is text pasted in wherever the macro is
                <em>used</em>.  The compiler reported the error at the
                definition, then told you where the paste happened:
                <c>note: in expansion of macro 'GPIOAEN'</c>, line 6.  Line 6 is
                the line it was really reading, and the missing semicolon is at
                the end of it, <em>below</em> line 2, not above.
            </p>
            <p>
                So the rule is to read up from the line the compiler was actually
                reading.  For ordinary code that is the reported line; when the
                message names a macro, it is the line given in the <c>note:</c>,
                which is wherever the macro was used.  Three rules recover almost
                every case:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li><em>Start at the top.</em>  One root error can spawn many
                    messages; the first one is closest to the cause, and fixing
                    it often clears several below it.</li>
                <li><em>Read up, from the right line.</em>  If the reported
                    line looks healthy, the bug is usually on an earlier line:
                    a missing semicolon, <c>)</c>, or <c>}</c> above.  But when
                    the message names a macro, the reported line is the
                    <c>#define</c>; read up from the line in the
                    <c>note: in expansion of macro</c> instead, which is
                    wherever the macro was used.</li>
                <li><em>Isolate when stuck.</em>  For a really pesky error,
                    comment out a block of code and rebuild.  If the error
                    moves, it tells you a bug was inside the commented out code.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                It is worth doing this once in your own project after today:
                delete that semicolon, build the project, read the message the
                compiler gives you, and then put the semicolon back.  Seeing the
                message your own compiler produces for a fault you created on
                purpose is what makes it recognizable the next time it appears by
                accident.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-debug-broken-blinky">
                <p>A classmate's Blinky that will not build.  Exactly <em>one</em> thing is wrong with it:</p>
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
#include "stm32c0xx.h"          // all register definitions live here
#define GPIOAEN  (1U << 0)      // RCC->IOPENR bit 0: clock for GPIOA
#define LED_PIN  (1U << 5)      // GPIOA bit 5: the on-board LED

int main(void) {
    RCC->IOPENR  |= GPIOAEN      // Step 1: enable GPIOA clock
    GPIOA->MODER |= (1U << 10);  // Step 2a: set   bit 10
    GPIOA->MODER &= ~(1U << 11); // Step 2b: clear bit 11 -> PA5 output

    while (1) {
        GPIOA->ODR |= LED_PIN;   // Step 3: bit 5 = 1 -> LED on
        for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++);
        GPIOA->ODR &= ~LED_PIN;  // Step 4: bit 5 = 0 -> LED off
        for (int i = 0; i < 100000; i++);
    }
    return 0;                    // never reached
}
]]></code></program>
            </slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-debug-transcript" room="yes">
                <p>The compiler's report.  (1) Read line 2: is anything wrong
                   with it?  (2) <em>Decide which line</em> the actual bug is on,
                   and what the fix is:</p>
                <program><code><![CDATA[
main.c: In function 'main':
main.c:2:18: error: called object is not a function or function pointer
    2 | #define GPIOAEN  (1U << 0)      // RCC->IOPENR bit 0: clock for GPIOA
      |                  ^
main.c:6:21: note: in expansion of macro 'GPIOAEN'
    6 |     RCC->IOPENR  |= GPIOAEN      // Step 1: enable GPIOA clock
      |                     ^~~~~~~
]]></code></program>
            </slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-debug-compiler-reveal">
                <ul>
                    <li>Line 2 is perfectly healthy.  The bug: the <em>missing semicolon</em> at the end of line 6.</li>
                    <li>With it gone, the compiler sees <c>GPIOAEN</c>'s value followed by a parenthesized expression (<c>GPIOA</c> is a macro too), and in C, a value followed by <c>( ... )</c> reads as a <em>function call</em>.  So the error points at the <q>called</q> value: the macro defined on line 2.</li>
                    <li>The reported line is where the compiler <em>gave up</em>.  Usually the mistake is at or <em>above</em> it, but not here.</li>
                    <li>A <c>#define</c> is pasted in wherever the macro is <em>used</em>, so the compiler reported the definition (line 2) and then said where the paste happened: <c>note: in expansion of macro</c>, <em>line 6</em>.  Line 6 is the line it was really reading, and the bug is <em>below</em> line 2, not above it.</li>
                </ul>
                <note>REVEAL — show after answers are committed.  This example is
                deliberately the exception to <q>read up</q>: make the point that
                the note, not the error, names the line to read up from.</note>
            </slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-debug-compiler-rules">
                <p>Three rules recover almost every compiler error:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><clr c="blue">Start at the top</clr>: one root error can spawn many messages; fixing the first often clears the rest.</li>
                    <li><clr c="blue">Read up, from the right line</clr>: when the reported line looks healthy, the mistake is usually at or above it.  But if the message names a macro, the reported line is the <c>#define</c>; read up from the <c>note: in expansion of macro</c> line instead, wherever the macro was used.  (That is today's example: <c>#define</c> on line 2, bug on line 6.)</li>
                    <li><clr c="blue">Isolate when stuck</clr>: comment out a block and rebuild.  If the error moves, a bug was inside the commented-out code.</li>
                </ul>
            </slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- ── Part 2b ────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-debug-part2b">
            <title>Part 2b: One printf, Placed Well</title>

            <!--
            INSTRUCTOR (5 min): predict-then-reveal.  Students commit to a
            placement before the framework is shown.  This generalizes the
            Day 7 "Nothing printed?" ladder.
            -->
            <p>
                When a program builds and runs but misbehaves, the fastest way to
                answer a question about it is a <c>printf</c>.  You have used
                <c>printf</c> since Day 5, so what we'll practice today is where to
                place one.
            </p>

            <activity xml:id="act-debug-printf-where">
                <title>Place One printf</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        The symptom: your <c>ADCPot.c</c> builds, flashes, and
                        runs: prints its two greeting lines, and then nothing
                        more, ever.
                    </p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-debug-printf-place">
                    <p>
                        You get to add exactly <em>one</em> <c>printf</c> to
                        <c>ADCPot.c</c>.  Decide which line it goes on.  There is
                        more than one good answer, but there are many less ideal
                        ones, so commit to yours before reading on.
                    </p>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-debug-printf-what">
                    <p>
                        Now decide what it prints.  A print that answers a
                        question is worth more than one that only says the
                        program got this far.
                    </p>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <!-- Reveal (after the activity) -->
            <p>
                A well-placed diagnostic <c>printf</c> answers one of three
                questions:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li><em>Did execution reach this point?</em>
                    <cd>printf("pa0_adc_init: entered\r\n");</cd>
                    The greeting printed, so the program runs and printing
                    works.  The hang is somewhere after it.  The highest-value
                    placement is the first line of <c>pa0_adc_init()</c>: it
                    splits <q>my init hangs</q> from <q>my sample loop
                    hangs</q>, and one more print halves the remaining half again.
                    That is bisection: each print cuts the region that can still
                    hold the fault in two.
                    (If nothing printed at all, not even the greeting,
                    the usual cause is the one we saw on Day 7: a
                    stray <c>blinky.c</c> or another unrelated program is what is actually building.)</li>
                <li><em>Is this value reasonable?</em>
                    <cd>printf("sensor_value = %d\r\n", sensor_value);</cd>
                    An ADC count of 0 or 4095 that never changes says
                    <em>configuration</em>; one that jitters near a plausible
                    value says the ADC is fine and the bug is downstream.</li>
                <li><em>Does the actual match the expected?</em>
                    <cd>printf("sensor_value = %d (expect 0..4095)\r\n", sensor_value);</cd>
                    Print what you expect <em>beside</em> what you got.  A number
                    with no expectation next to it is easy to wave through.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Two housekeeping rules from Day 5 still apply: end every printf() string
                with <c>\r\n</c>, and remember that a 32-bit register needs
                <c>%08lx</c> (<xref ref="subsec-debug-printf-patterns"/> collects
                the patterns).  And when the bug is found: take the diagnostic
                prints out.  They slow the loop and clutter the output of the
                finished program.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-debug-printf-reveal">
                <p>A well-placed diagnostic <c>printf</c> answers <em>one</em> of three questions:</p>
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
/* 1. Did execution reach this point?  The greeting printed,
      so printing works -- the hang is after it.  This print
      splits "init hangs" from "loop hangs".  Then bisect.   */
printf("pa0_adc_init: entered\r\n");

/* 2. Is this value reasonable?  Stuck at 0 or 4095 = config;
      jitter near a plausible value = look downstream.       */
printf("sensor_value = %d\r\n", sensor_value);

/* 3. Does ACTUAL match EXPECTED?  Print them side by side.  */
printf("sensor_value = %d (expect 0..4095)\r\n", sensor_value);
]]></code></program>
                <ul>
                    <li>One print cuts the program in half.  Bisection corners the bug in a few builds.</li>
                    <li><c>\r\n</c> at the end; a 32-bit register prints with <c>%08lx</c> (Day 5).</li>
                    <li>Bug fixed → diagnostic prints come out.</li>
                </ul>
                <note>REVEAL — show after placements are committed.</note>
            </slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-debug-printf-solution" instructor="yes">
                <p>One concrete answer for <c>ADCPot.c</c>:</p>
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
void pa0_adc_init(void) {
    printf("pa0_adc_init: entered\r\n");   // <-- the one printf
    ...
]]></code></program>
                <ul>
                    <li>If it prints and then silence: the hang is <em>after</em> init, and the usual culprits are the two <c>while</c> loops that wait on a flag &#8212; the one waiting for <c>ADRDY</c> in <c>start_conversion()</c>, and the one waiting for <c>EOC</c> in <c>adc_read()</c>.  A second print at the top of <c>adc_read()</c> decides which.</li>
                    <li>If it never prints: the hang is before init, rare in this file, and now you know.</li>
                </ul>
            </slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- ── Part 3a ────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-debug-part3a">
            <title>Part 3a: Launching the Debugger</title>

            <!--
            INSTRUCTOR (8 min): the launch.  Say out loud: "a good number of
            these won't launch on the first try — that's normal.  Put your hand
            up and I'll come by."  Sit on the ladder slide while circulating.
            -->
            <p>
                The third tool is the <term>interactive debugger</term> built into
                the STM32CubeIDE, and it does something the other two cannot: it
                halts the processor wherever you ask, and then lets you look at any
                variable and any peripheral register on the chip, and step the
                program forward one line at a time.  A <c>printf</c> only reports
                from the places you decided in advance to put one; the debugger
                needs no such decision beforehand.  It talks to the STM32C031C6
                over the same USB cable you already program with (the Nucleo's
                ST-LINK circuitry is the go-between), and it works even before
                the UART does.  The debugger finds errors that appear while the
                program runs, not compile errors, so the project has to compile and
                build without errors first.
            </p>
            <p>
                The launch itself is one click: the <em>bug icon</em> in the
                toolbar, immediately left of the green Run button
                (<xref ref="fig-debug-bug-icon"/>).
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-debug-bug-icon">
                <caption>The bug icon (boxed) is beside the green Run button
                in the toolbar.  Clicking it builds, flashes, and launches the
                program under the debugger.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day07x-Debugging/toolbar_bug_icon.png" width="45%"/>
            </figure>

            <activity xml:id="act-debug-launch">
                <title>Launch a Debug Session</title>
                <task xml:id="task-debug-launch-build">
                    <p>
                        Your <c>Blinky</c> project should be open and built from
                        the start of class.  Run it normally once to confirm the
                        on-board LED still blinks.
                    </p>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-debug-launch-bug">
                    <p>
                        Click the <em>bug icon</em> in the toolbar (next to the
                        green Run button, the boxed icon in the figure)
                        to build, flash, and launch under the debugger.  When
                        the <q>Confirm perspective switch</q> window appears,
                        accept it.
                    </p>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-debug-launch-compare">
                    <p>
                        Check your screen against the debug-perspective
                        screenshot.  The layout has changed (this is the
                        <term>debug perspective</term>) and execution is halted
                        at the first statement of <c>main()</c>, highlighted in
                        blue.  The program is loaded but has not run a single
                        line of your code yet.
                    </p>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <figure xml:id="fig-debug-perspective">
                <caption>The debug perspective, immediately after launch.  The
                editor holds the Blinky source, <c>blinky.c</c> in this capture,
                with the first statement of
                <c>main()</c> highlighted in blue, loaded, but not yet
                executed.  The tabbed pane on the right is the one we'll work in:
                the SFRs tab first, and the Variables tab later in the hour (the
                tab labels truncate on screen to <q>Variabl</q> and
                <q>Breakp</q>).</caption>
                <image source="images/Day07x-Debugging/slide15_img2.png" width="95%"/>
            </figure>

            <insight xml:id="insight-debug-ladder">
                <title>If the Debugger Will Not Launch</title>
                <p>
                    Four failure modes cover nearly every stuck launch.  Find
                    your symptom below and apply the fix.  A launch that does not
                    come up on the first try is normal.
                </p>
                <ul>
                    <li><em>The project will not build.</em>  You are likely trying to debug
                        some other code than the known and already working Blinky. Download the known-good
                        <url href="external/starters/blinky.c"><c>blinky.c</c></url>
                        (also on Canvas, first day), replace the contents of your
                        project's <c>Src/main.c</c> with it, and build again.  
                        If the project <em>still</em> will not
                        build, raise your hand and watch with a neighbor: nothing
                        later today requires your own launch, and you can
                        redo this walkthrough from this chapter, step by step,
                        before the next class.</li>
                    <li><em>Board not found, or a connection error at
                        launch.</em>  Reseat the USB cable at both ends, and close
                        any other program that might be holding the board (a
                        CoolTerm window on the same port, or an ST-LINK utility).</li>
                    <li><em>Clicked the wrong button on the perspective
                        popup.</em>  Window <m>\to</m> Perspective <m>\to</m>
                        Open Perspective <m>\to</m> Debug brings the debug
                        layout up by hand.</li>
                    <li><em>The session behaves strangely, or a second launch
                        fails.</em>  A previous debug session is probably still
                        running.  Terminate it with the red square first:
                        <em>one debug session at a time</em>.</li>
                </ul>
            </insight>

            <slide xml:id="sl-debug-bug-icon" ref="fig-debug-bug-icon">
                <ul>
                    <li>Blinky built?  Run it once normally.  The LED should blink.</li>
                    <li>Then click the <em>bug icon</em> (boxed), beside the green Run button, to build, flash, and launch under the debugger.</li>
                    <li>Accept the <q>Confirm perspective switch</q> popup.</li>
                </ul>
                <caption>One click: the bug icon launches the debugger.</caption>
            </slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-debug-ladder">
                <p>If the debugger will not launch, find your symptom.  A launch
                   that does not come up on the first try is normal:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><em>Project won't build</em> → you are likely debugging other code than the known-good Blinky: download <c>blinky.c</c> (Canvas, first day, or the link in this chapter), paste it over <c>Src/main.c</c>, build again.  Still stuck?  We're here to help!</li>
                    <li><em>Board not found / connection error</em> → reseat the USB cable at both ends, and close anything else holding the board (a CoolTerm window on the same port, or an ST-LINK utility).</li>
                    <li><em>Wrong click on the perspective popup</em> → Window → Perspective → Open Perspective → Debug.</li>
                    <li><em>Second launch fails / session acts strangely</em> → a previous session is still running: Terminate (red square) first.  <em>One debug session at a time.</em></li>
                </ul>
            </slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-debug-perspective" ref="fig-debug-perspective">
                <caption>Your screen now: the debug perspective, halted at the first line of main(), highlighted blue, loaded, nothing run yet.</caption>
            </slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- ── Part 3b ────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-debug-part3b">
            <title>Part 3b: Watching a Register Change</title>

            <!--
            INSTRUCTOR (5 min): the crucial step.  Prediction in writing BEFORE
            the step.  Checkpoint: by minute 32 the room should be past this.
            -->
            <p>
                Your debug session from <xref ref="subsec-debug-part3a"/> is
                halted at the first line of <c>main()</c>, and while it is
                halted, the debugger can show you every peripheral register on
                the chip, live.  In the pane on the right, select the
                <term>SFRs</term> tab (<em>Special Function
                Registers</em>).  It lists the processor's internals under
                <c>Cortex_M0PLUS</c> and every peripheral you have used, under
                <c>STM32C031</c>: ADC, GPIOA, RCC, USART2, and the rest, each
                expandable down to individual registers and bit fields
                (<xref ref="fig-debug-sfr-tree"/>).  These are the same
                registers, with the same names, as in the reference manual,
                <url href="external/stm32c031_rm.pdf">RM0490</url>.  What the SFRs
                view adds is the value each one holds on your own chip at this
                moment.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-debug-sfr-tree">
                <caption>The SFRs tab: the processor's internals under
                Cortex_M0PLUS, and every peripheral from the reference manual
                under STM32C031.  The <c>x16</c>/<c>x10</c>/<c>x2</c> buttons
                switch the value display's radix between base-16 (hexadecimal),
                base-10 (decimal) and base-2 (binary).</caption>
                <image source="images/Day07x-Debugging/slide16_img1.png" width="65%"/>
            </figure>

            <p>
                The first statement of <c>main()</c> (highlighted in blue,
                loaded, not yet executed) is one you can recite from Day 1:
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
RCC->IOPENR |= GPIOAEN;     // Step 1: enable GPIOA clock
]]></code></program>

            <activity xml:id="act-debug-iopenr">
                <title>Predict, Then Step</title>
                <task xml:id="task-debug-iopenr-expand">
                    <p>
                        In the SFRs tab, expand <c>RCC</c>, then
                        <c>RCC_IOPENR</c>.  Note the current value of the
                        register, and take a look at its bit 0 (the field named
                        <c>GPIOAEN</c>).
                    </p>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-debug-iopenr-predict">
                    <p>
                        The blue-highlighted line is about to run.  Predict what
                        <c>RCC_IOPENR</c> will read <em>after</em> it executes, the
                        whole register, not just the bit.
                    </p>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-debug-iopenr-step">
                    <p>
                        Execute exactly that one line: click <em>Step Over</em>
                        in the toolbar.  Check <c>RCC_IOPENR</c> against your
                        prediction.
                    </p>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <figure xml:id="fig-debug-iopenr">
                <caption>RCC_IOPENR before and after single-stepping the first
                line of <c>main()</c>.  Left: at launch the register reads 0x0,
                and GPIOA's clock is off.  Right: after one Step Over it reads 0x1,
                and the GPIOAEN field holds 1.  The yellow highlight means
                <q>this value changed since the debugger last looked.</q>
                Right after launch the debugger is reading every register for
                the first time, so many rows glow yellow.  Ignore that first
                flush.  After a step the highlight becomes useful: only
                RCC_IOPENR is yellow, the one register the step changed.</caption>
                <sidebyside widths="48% 48%" margins="1%">
                    <image source="images/Day07x-Debugging/iopenr_before_tight.png"/>
                    <image source="images/Day07x-Debugging/slide17_img2.png"/>
                </sidebyside>
            </figure>

            <p>
                You wrote <c>RCC-&gt;IOPENR |= GPIOAEN;</c> in week one and trusted
                that it did something.  You have now <em>watched it happen</em> on
                your own board: one line of code changing one bit in one register.
                Every register write in every
                program this term can be checked the same way.
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-debug-sfrs" ref="fig-debug-sfr-tree">
                <ul>
                    <li>The <em>SFRs</em> tab lists the processor's internals under <c>Cortex_M0PLUS</c>, and every peripheral you have used under <c>STM32C031</c>.</li>
                    <li>Expand a peripheral → its registers, addresses, and current values; expand a register → its bit fields.</li>
                    <li><c>x16</c> / <c>x10</c> / <c>x2</c> switch the display between hex, decimal, and binary.</li>
                    <li>The register names here are the same as in the reference manual, RM0490, and the value beside each one is what your own chip holds right now.</li>
                </ul>
                <caption>Select the SFRs tab in the right-hand pane, then expand RCC.</caption>
            </slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-debug-iopenr-reveal" ref="fig-debug-iopenr">
                <caption>One Step Over: RCC_IOPENR 0x0 → 0x1, GPIOAEN 0 → 1.  At launch the debugger reads everything for the first time, so many rows glow yellow; ignore that first flush.  After the step, IOPENR is the only yellow row: the one register that changed.  This is the line you wrote in week one, now watched as it executes.</caption>
            </slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- ── Part 3c ────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-debug-part3c">
            <title>Part 3c: The Register That Was Not Zero</title>

            <!--
            INSTRUCTOR (4 min): observe -> puzzle -> RM crop -> punchline.
            The decode is TOLD (punchline), not derived by the class.
            -->
            <p>
                Blinky's next two lines, the ones you have not stepped yet
                (<xref ref="subsec-debug-part3b"/>), configure PA5 as an
                output.  Before stepping them, expand <c>GPIOA</c> and look at
                <c>GPIOA_MODER</c>, and look closely, because the value is not
                zero: it reads <c>0xEBFFFFFF</c>, and nearly every
                MODE field, including <c>MODE5</c> for our LED pin, holds
                <c>0x3</c> (<xref ref="fig-debug-moder-before"/>).
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-debug-moder-before">
                <caption>GPIOA_MODER before the two MODER lines run: the
                register reads 0xEBFFFFFF, and MODE5, like almost every MODE
                field, holds 0x3.  Why is a register the program has never
                touched not zero?</caption>
                <image source="images/Day07x-Debugging/slide17_img3.png" width="70%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                Why?  This is a question for the reference manual, and it is one
                you have looked up before: in the Day 3 activity you went to
                <url href="external/stm32c031_rm.pdf">RM0490</url> §6.4.1 for
                MODER's power-on default.  The answer
                (<xref ref="fig-rm-moder-reset"/>): the reset value is
                0xEBFF FFFF for port A and 0xFFFF FFFF for every other port:
                pins wake up in <em>analog</em> mode (<c>11</c>), the
                high-impedance do-nothing state you saw when configuring the ADC
                on Day 7.  Today you are watching that sentence of the manual be
                true on live hardware.
            </p>
            <p>
                And the <c>EB</c> at the top of port A's value?  Two pins do
                <em>not</em> reset to analog: PA13 and PA14 wake up in
                alternate-function mode.  They are SWDIO and SWCLK, the serial
                wire debug port.  The debugger you are using at this very moment
                is connected through those two pins, and if they reset to analog
                like the rest, a fresh chip could never be debugged.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-rm-moder-reset">
                <caption>RM0490 §6.4.1, <em>GPIO port mode register
                (GPIOx_MODER)</em>: reset value 0xEBFF FFFF for port A,
                0xFFFF FFFF for the other ports.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day07x-Debugging/rm_moder_reset_value.png" width="80%"/>
            </figure>

            <p>
                Now step through the two MODER lines and watch <c>MODE5</c> go
                from <c>0x3</c> (analog) to <c>0x1</c> (output,
                <xref ref="fig-debug-moder-after"/>), and notice it takes
                <em>both</em> lines to get there: <c>|= (1U&lt;&lt;10)</c>
                alone would leave <c>11</c> as <c>11</c>, which is exactly why
                Blinky clears bit 11 explicitly.  Notice also what you just
                did: you <em>audited</em> a register write: every bit the code
                claims to set, read back set.  This works on any
                register, in any program.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-debug-moder-after">
                <caption>GPIOA_MODER after stepping both MODER lines: the
                register reads 0xEBFFF7FF and MODE5 holds 0x1.  PA5 is now an
                output.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day07x-Debugging/slide17_img4.png" width="70%"/>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-debug-moder-observe" ref="fig-debug-moder-before" stack="yes">
                <p>Before stepping the MODER lines, expand <c>GPIOA_MODER</c> in the SFRs view.  It reads <c>0xEBFFFFFF</c>.</p>
                <p><term>Why is this register not zero, when your program has never touched it?</term></p>
            </slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-debug-moder-rm" ref="fig-rm-moder-reset" stack="yes">
                <ul>
                    <li>We looked this up on Day 3: RM0490 §6.4.1 gives the reset value <c>0xEBFF FFFF</c> for port A and <c>0xFFFF FFFF</c> for the other ports.</li>
                    <li>Pins wake up <em>analog</em> (<c>11</c>), the high-impedance do-nothing state from Day 7.  That is the <c>0x3</c> in every MODE field.</li>
                    <li>The <c>EB</c>: PA13/PA14 reset to <em>alternate function</em> instead, SWDIO and SWCLK, the debug port.  The debugger you are using right now depends on those two pins.</li>
                </ul>
                <caption>The reset value, verified on live hardware.</caption>
            </slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-debug-moder-step" ref="fig-debug-moder-after" stack="yes">
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
GPIOA->MODER |= (1U << 10);   // Step 2a: set   bit 10
GPIOA->MODER &= ~(1U << 11);  // Step 2b: clear bit 11 -> PA5 output
]]></code></program>
                <p>Step both lines and watch <c>MODE5</c> go from <c>0x3</c> to <c>0x1</c>.  It takes both of them: the <c>|=</c> alone leaves <c>11</c> as <c>11</c>.  You have just audited a register write, and every bit the code claims to set reads back set.</p>
            </slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- ── Part 3d ────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-debug-part3d">
            <title>Part 3d: Breakpoints</title>

            <!--
            INSTRUCTOR (7 min): bridge from stepping, then the LED/ODR hop.
            If the minute-32 checkpoint fired, this runs as a student-driven
            front demo instead of individual hands-on.
            -->
            <p>
                Single-stepping is precise but slow, and you must <em>not</em>
                step your way into Blinky's delay loop.  Instead of walking the
                program line by line, you can mark a line of interest with a
                <term>breakpoint</term> and let the program run at full speed
                until it reaches it.  The statements in between execute normally,
                but the program pauses when it gets to a breakpoint so that you
                can look at the registers and the variables.  Setting two
                breakpoints
                (<xref ref="fig-debug-breakpoints"/>) and watching
                <c>GPIOA_ODR</c> at each stop (<xref ref="fig-debug-odr"/>) is
                the exercise below.
            </p>

            <activity xml:id="act-debug-breakpoints">
                <title>Run Between Two Breakpoints</title>
                <task xml:id="task-debug-bp-set">
                    <p>
                        Place a breakpoint beside the line that turns the
                        LED on (<c>GPIOA-&gt;ODR |= LED_PIN;</c>) and another one beside the line that
                        turns the LED off (right-click the thin margin strip at the left edge of the code
                        window beside the row where you wish to add the breakpoint).  A small marker appears beside
                        each.
                    </p>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-debug-bp-resume">
                    <p>
                        Click <em>Resume</em>.  The delay loop executes at full
                        speed and the program suspends at the next breakpoint.
                        Click Resume a few more times, watching two things at
                        each stop: the on-board LED, and <c>OD5</c> inside
                        <c>GPIOA_ODR</c> in the SFRs view.
                    </p>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-debug-bp-connect">
                    <p>
                        At each stop, check: does the LED's state agree with
                        <c>OD5</c>?  You are hopping the program between its
                        two visible states, with the register view and the
                        hardware in lockstep.
                    </p>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <figure xml:id="fig-debug-breakpoints">
                <caption>Two breakpoints set in the left margin, on the
                LED-on and LED-off lines of Blinky's loop.  Each Resume runs the
                delay loop at full speed and suspends at the next marked
                line.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day07x-Debugging/slide19_img1.png" width="90%"/>
            </figure>

            <figure xml:id="fig-debug-odr">
                <caption>GPIOA_ODR at the two breakpoints.  Left: 0x0 with
                OD5 = 0, the LED is off.  Right: 0x20 with OD5 = 1, the LED
                is on.  The register view and the physical LED change
                together.</caption>
                <sidebyside widths="48% 48%" margins="1%">
                    <image source="images/Day07x-Debugging/slide18_img1.png"/>
                    <image source="images/Day07x-Debugging/slide18_img2.png"/>
                </sidebyside>
            </figure>

            <p>
                If you do find yourself trapped mid-delay-loop (it happens),
                do not click your way out.  Use <em>Terminate and Relaunch</em>
                to start the session fresh, or <em>Reset the chip and restart
                execution</em>; both are on the toolbar
                (<xref ref="subsec-debug-controls"/> has the full icon
                reference).
            </p>

            <slide xml:id="sl-debug-breakpoints" ref="fig-debug-breakpoints" stack="yes">
                <ul>
                    <li>Stepping every line is slow, and never step <em>into</em> the delay loop.  Set a destination instead.</li>
                    <li>Right-click the thin left margin beside a line → <q>Add Breakpoint</q>.  The program runs at full speed until it gets there, then pauses so you can look at the registers and the variables.</li>
                    <li>Trapped mid-loop anyway?  <em>Terminate and Relaunch</em>, or <em>Reset the chip and restart execution</em>.</li>
                </ul>
                <caption>Two breakpoints: on the LED-on line and the LED-off line.</caption>
            </slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-debug-odr-reveal" ref="fig-debug-odr" stack="yes">
                <ul>
                    <li>Place a breakpoint beside the LED-on line (<c>GPIOA-&gt;ODR |= LED_PIN;</c>) and another one beside the LED-off line (<c>GPIOA-&gt;ODR &amp;= ~LED_PIN;</c>): right-click the thin margin strip at the left edge of the code window, beside the row where you want the breakpoint.</li>
                    <li>Click <em>Resume</em>.  The delay loop runs at full speed and the program stops at the next breakpoint.</li>
                    <li>At each stop, check two things: the on-board LED, and <c>OD5</c> inside <c>GPIOA_ODR</c> in the SFRs view.  Do they agree?</li>
                </ul>
                <caption>Left 0x0 with OD5 = 0, the LED is off; right 0x20 with OD5 = 1, the LED is on.</caption>
            </slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- ── Part 4 ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-debug-part4">
            <title>Part 4: Watching a Variable Change</title>

            <!--
            INSTRUCTOR (5 min): default is all-hands — everyone predicts, then
            presses.  If pressed for time, switch to demo mode from the front
            (see the instructor reminder slide); the class still writes the
            prediction before the keypress.
            -->
            <p>
                Registers are half the story; the debugger watches your C
                variables just as easily, in the <term>Variables</term> tab of
                the same right-hand pane.  The program is Day 5's
                keyboard-controlled counter, whose receive path polls the RXNE
                (receive data register not empty) flag and reads the character
                register only when a byte has
                arrived:
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
while (1) {
    if (USART2->ISR & USART_ISR_RXNE_RXFNE) {
        char key = USART2->RDR & 0xFF;      // <-- breakpoint goes here
        if      (key == 'u' || key == 'U') count++;
        else if (key == 'd' || key == 'D') count--;
        else if (key == 'r' || key == 'R') count = 0;
    }
    printf("count = %d\r\n", count);
    delay_ms(500);
}
]]></code></program>
            <p>
                A breakpoint on the marked line is useful because that line is
                reached only when the RXNE test succeeds.
                So the program runs free, printing <c>count</c> twice a second,
                until the moment a key is pressed in CoolTerm, and then it
                halts with the fresh byte one step away.  A breakpoint placed
                inside an <c>if</c> means <em>break when this happens</em>.
            </p>

            <activity xml:id="act-debug-ascii-predict">
                <title>Predict the Value, Then Press the Key</title>
                <task xml:id="task-debug-ascii-setup">
                    <p>
                        Terminate the Blinky session (red square), open your
                        keyboard-counter project from Day 5, and launch it
                        under the debugger.  Open CoolTerm as usual, set a
                        breakpoint on the marked line, and click Resume.  The
                        counter prints away, and the breakpoint waits.
                    </p>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-debug-ascii">
                    <p>
                        Before pressing anything: predict the
                        <em>decimal</em> value the variable
                        <c>key</c> will hold after the marked line executes when
                        you press <c>u</c>.  Anchor: <c>'a'</c> is 97, and the
                        lowercase letters run in order.
                    </p>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-debug-ascii-press">
                    <p>
                        Now press <c>u</c> in CoolTerm.  The program halts at
                        the breakpoint; click Step Over once to execute the
                        read, and check <c>key</c> in the Variables tab against
                        your prediction.  Then find <c>USART_RDR</c> in the
                        SFRs view.
                    </p>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <p>
                What you'll see on screen: after the keypress lands and one Step
                Over executes the read, the Variables tab shows <c>key</c>
                holding 117, and the SFRs view shows <c>USART_RDR</c> holding
                0x75 (<xref ref="fig-debug-variables"/>).  Same byte, three
                names: the character <c>'u'</c>, the decimal 117, the hex 0x75.
                The Variables tab shows you that byte as your C code sees it, and
                the SFRs tab shows the same byte in the peripheral register it
                arrived in.
            </p>

            <figure xml:id="fig-debug-variables">
                <caption>The same keypress in two views.  Left: the Variables
                tab after stepping the read, <c>key</c> holds 117 (this
                capture is from a counter variant whose <c>key</c> is an
                <c>int</c> and whose count is an <c>int8_t</c> named
                <c>counter</c>; the values, not the names or types, are the
                point).  Both rows glow yellow here (the debugger has just
                refreshed them), so read the values, not the colors:
                <c>key</c> is the one that changed.  Right: USART2's
                receive data register <c>USART_RDR</c> in the SFRs view holds
                0x75.  117 and 0x75 are the same number, the ASCII code for
                <c>'u'</c>.</caption>
                <sidebyside widths="48% 48%" margins="1%">
                    <image source="images/Day07x-Debugging/slide21_img1.png"/>
                    <image source="images/Day07x-Debugging/slide21_img2.png"/>
                </sidebyside>
            </figure>

            <slide xml:id="sl-debug-part4-mode" instructor="yes">
                <ul>
                    <li>Reminder to self: if pressed for time, run Part 4 as a demo from the front instead of all-hands: the staged counter + CoolTerm on the podium machine.</li>
                    <li>Either way, everyone writes the prediction <em>before</em> the keypress.</li>
                </ul>
            </slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-debug-counter-bp">
                <p>Day 5's keyboard counter, under the debugger.  The receive
                   path only runs when a byte has actually arrived:</p>
                <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
while (1) {
    if (USART2->ISR & USART_ISR_RXNE_RXFNE) {
        char key = USART2->RDR & 0xFF;      // <-- breakpoint goes here
        if      (key == 'u' || key == 'U') count++;
        else if (key == 'd' || key == 'D') count--;
        else if (key == 'r' || key == 'R') count = 0;
    }
    printf("count = %d\r\n", count);
    delay_ms(500);
}
]]></code></program>
                <ul>
                    <li>A breakpoint <em>inside the if</em> means <q>break when this happens</q>: the program runs free, printing <c>count</c> twice a second, until a key lands in CoolTerm.</li>
                </ul>
            </slide>

            <slide xml:id="sl-debug-variables-reveal" ref="fig-debug-variables">
                <caption>key = 117 in Variables; USART_RDR = 0x75 in SFRs.  One byte, three names: 'u', 117, 0x75.</caption>
            </slide>
        </subsection>

        <!-- ── Part 5 ─────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
        <subsection xml:id="subsec-debug-part5">
            <title>Part 5: Choosing the Right Tool</title>

            <!--
            INSTRUCTOR (3 min): the map, the stretch, the re-entry path.
            Then the rest of the hour is Lab 4 with the debugger open.
            -->
            <p>
                Each of these tools answers a different kind of question.  The
                table below matches a symptom to the tool to reach for:
            </p>

            <table xml:id="table-debug-tool-map">
                <title>Which tool, when</title>
                <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                        <cell>Symptom</cell>
                        <cell>Reach for</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Will not build</cell>
                        <cell>Compiler messages: top down, read up</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Runs, but a value is wrong or missing</cell>
                        <cell><c>printf</c> placed to answer one question</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Need to see a register or step a line</cell>
                        <cell>Debugger: SFRs, stepping, breakpoints</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Suspect the circuit, not the code</cell>
                        <cell>Digital multimeter (DMM) and Analog Discovery 2 (AD2); the checklist is in the Reference section</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </table>

            <p>
                A breakpoint <em>stops the processor</em>, so anything that
                depends on real-time behavior stops with it: a paused chip cannot
                blink, sample, or keep time.  For a timing question the
                oscilloscope is the better tool, because it watches the pins
                without pausing the program.  We'll come back to this when we
                start using interrupts.
            </p>
            <p>
                These tools are also how you check code you did not write,
                including code an AI tool drafted for you.  The
                course policy (<xref ref="aside-ai-policy"/>) asks you to be
                able to explain every line you submit; the compiler's report,
                a well-placed <c>printf</c>, and a register audit in the
                debugger are the tooling that lets you find out whether you
                can.
            </p>
            <p>
                You can now apply all of this in Lab 4.
            </p>

            <activity xml:id="act-debug-stretch">
                <title>Stretch: Debug Your Actual Lab</title>
                <introduction>
                    <p>
                        Finished the walkthrough with time to spare?  Use the
                        same tools on your own Lab 4 program.
                    </p>
                </introduction>
                <task xml:id="task-debug-stretch-dr">
                    <p>
                        Launch your <c>ADCPot.c</c> under the debugger.  Set a
                        breakpoint on the line after the EOC (end of conversion)
                        wait, and watch
                        <c>ADC_DR</c> in the SFRs view while you turn the
                        potentiometer between stops.
                    </p>
                </task>
                <task xml:id="task-debug-stretch-audit">
                    <p>
                        Then audit your own initialization: halt after
                        <c>pa0_adc_init()</c> and check <c>ADC_CHSELR</c> and
                        <c>ADC_CR</c> against what your code claims to have
                        written.  Does every bit you set actually read back
                        set?</p>
                </task>
            </activity>

            <!-- The slide carries its own copy of the tabular rather than ref'ing
                 table-debug-tool-map: a ref'd table keeps its <title> on the wall
                 (the player shows it deliberately), and "Which tool, when" is a
                 duplicate of this slide's own title.  Her comment, 2026-09-15:
                 "delete".  Keep the two in step if the book table changes. -->
            <slide xml:id="sl-debug-tool-map">
                <p>Find your symptom in the left column, and the right column names the tool to reach for.</p>
                <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                    <col width="40%"/>
                    <col width="60%"/>
                    <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
                        <cell>Symptom</cell>
                        <cell>Reach for</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Will not build</cell>
                        <cell>Compiler messages: top down, read up</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Runs, but a value is wrong or missing</cell>
                        <cell><c>printf</c> placed to answer one question</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Need to see a register or step a line</cell>
                        <cell>Debugger: SFRs, stepping, breakpoints</cell>
                    </row>
                    <row>
                        <cell>Suspect the circuit, not the code</cell>
                        <cell>Digital multimeter (DMM) and Analog Discovery 2 (AD2); the checklist is in the Reference section</cell>
                    </row>
                </tabular>
            </slide>
        </subsection>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ -->
    <section xml:id="sec-debug-reference">
        <title>Reference: The Debugging Method and Toolbox</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                Everything in this section is lookup material, written to come back
                to when a bug has you stuck mid-lab.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-debug-method">
            <title>The Scientific Debugging Method</title>
            <p>
                Effective debugging is a systematic process, and it has five
                steps, which are best taken in order:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li><em>Reproduce the problem reliably.</em>  A bug you cannot
                    reproduce is almost impossible to fix.  Note the exact
                    inputs, conditions, and sequence that trigger it.</li>
                <li><em>Form a specific hypothesis.</em>  <q>Something is wrong
                    with the ADC</q> is not a hypothesis; <q>the ADC clock is
                    never enabled, so the data register always reads zero</q>
                    is.  It names a cause you can test.</li>
                <li><em>Change one thing at a time.</em>  If you change five
                    things and the bug disappears, you do not know which change
                    mattered.  The root cause is still unknown, and the bug is
                    free to return.</li>
                <li><em>Bisect.</em>  Confirm the program is healthy at its
                    midpoint (a <c>printf</c>, a breakpoint), then narrow to
                    the half that contains the fault.  Each test halves the
                    search.</li>
                <li><em>Keep notes.</em>  Once you have been stuck for more than a
                    few minutes, keep a record of what you tried and what you
                    observed.  It prevents
                    re-testing the same idea, and it is exactly what a TA or
                    instructor will ask you for.</li>
            </ul>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-debug-prevention">
            <title>Preventing Bugs</title>
            <p>
                The bugs that cost the least time are the ones that never happen,
                and the practices below are the ones that prevent them:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>Plan the design on paper (block diagrams, pseudocode,
                    state diagrams) before writing code.</li>
                <li>Use the file header to record what the file is, the
                    hardware it expects, and its history.</li>
                <li>Write in small chunks and test as you go.</li>
                <li>Strive for readable code, with descriptive names.</li>
                <li>Use functions.  A good function has one clear objective:
                    <q>set up the UART</q>, <q>get a value from the ADC</q>.</li>
                <li>Comment as you go, to narrate your process and leave notes
                    for the next person.</li>
                <li>Use named constants (<c>const</c> or <c>#define</c>) rather
                    than literal <q>magic numbers</q>.</li>
                <li>Delete commented-out code from the final version.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                One more habit belongs on this list, and it is about choosing a
                loop.  Use <c>for</c> when the number of iterations is known in
                advance, <c>for (i = 0; i &lt; N; i++)</c>, and <c>while</c> (or
                <c>do ... while</c>) when it is not.  C does allow
                <c>break</c> to exit a <c>for</c> loop early, but if you find
                yourself reaching for it, a <c>while</c> loop is usually the better
                fit.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-debug-hardware">
            <title>The Hardware Checklist</title>
            <p>
                When the circuit is suspect, check the wiring <em>before</em>
                applying power:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>Power and ground connected to the correct pins on every
                    device?  On the Nucleo, breadboard power comes from the
                    <em>3V3</em> pin, not the <em>NRST</em> pin directly above
                    it (<xref ref="fig-nucleo-power-header"/>).  Power on NRST
                    does not damage anything, but it drives the chip's reset
                    pin, and the debugger will refuse to connect until the wire
                    moves.</li>
                <li>Bypass capacitors installed?</li>
                <li>LED polarity correct?</li>
                <li>Wires pushed into the breadboard all the way?</li>
                <li>Does each wire begin and end where the circuit diagram says
                    it should?</li>
            </ul>

            <figure xml:id="fig-nucleo-power-header">
                <caption>The Nucleo power header.  NRST is directly above
                3.3 V, an easy mis-plug.  Breadboard power comes from the
                3.3 V pin.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day07x-Debugging/nucleo_power_header.png" width="35%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                If (and only if) the wiring checks out, apply power.  Remove
                power again before making changes.  Then measure rather than
                assume, with the DMM and the AD2 oscilloscope from Day 3:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>Is each chip getting power?  Measure at the chip's own
                    pins, not at the rail.</li>
                <li>Do the UART signals look right on the scope?</li>
                <li>Are sensor outputs in the range the datasheet promises?</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                The AD2 has one more debugging capability, which we'll use in a
                later chapter: its logic analyzer can decode digital protocols, displaying the
                actual bytes on a UART or I2C line alongside the waveform.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-debug-printf-patterns">
            <title>Diagnostic printf Patterns</title>
            <p>
                The three diagnostic questions from class, as copy-ready
                patterns (format specifiers are Day 5 material; the full table
                is in <xref ref="sec-uart-driver" text="title-element"/>):
            </p>
            <program language="c"><code><![CDATA[
/* Did execution reach this point? */
printf("pa0_adc_init: entered\r\n");
printf("made it past the config loop\r\n");

/* Is this value reasonable?  (decimal and hex of the same value) */
printf("ADC value: %u\t0x%x\r\n", value, value);

/* Does actual match expected?  Print them side by side. */
printf("count = %d (expect 0..4095)\r\n", count);

/* What does that I/O register actually hold?  32-bit needs %08lx. */
printf("GPIOA->ODR = 0x%08lx\r\n", GPIOA->ODR);
]]></code></program>
            <!-- check-rules: allow L-2  (this paragraph teaches the %f caveat) -->
            <p>
                Remember from Day 5: every line ends in <c>\r\n</c>; a 16-bit
                value prints with <c>%x</c>, a 32-bit register with
                <c>%08lx</c>.  Floating-point <c>printf</c> is off by default
                in the course library; Lab 4 Appendix A shows the one project
                setting that enables it.  Even then, prefer scaling to an
                integer and printing <c>%d</c>: the STM32C031C6 has no floating point hardware, so
                every float operation is emulated in software.  Remove
                diagnostic prints once the bug is fixed.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-debug-controls">
            <title>Debugger Controls</title>
            <p>
                The debug toolbar's icons are collected in
                <xref ref="fig-debug-controls"/>.  The three used constantly:
                <em>Resume</em> (run to the next breakpoint), <em>Step Over</em>
                (execute one line, without descending into function calls), and
                <em>Terminate</em> (end the session).  <em>Step Into</em>
                descends into a called function; <em>Terminate and Relaunch</em>
                and <em>Reset the chip and restart execution</em> are the two
                escape hatches when a session goes sideways.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-debug-controls">
                <caption>The main debugger control icons in STM32CubeIDE:
                resume, halt, the three step variants (into / over / out),
                C-versus-instruction stepping, reset-and-restart, terminate,
                and terminate-and-relaunch.</caption>
                <image source="images/Day07x-Debugging/slide20_f789c46b.png" width="80%"/>
            </figure>
            <p>
                Two habits keep sessions healthy.  Run <em>one debug session at
                a time</em>.  Most of the debugger's mysterious misbehavior
                traces to a previous session left running; terminate before you
                relaunch.  And never single-step into a long software delay
                loop; if you land in one, Terminate and Relaunch is faster than
                clicking your way out.
            </p>
        </subsection>

        <subsection xml:id="subsec-debug-tools">
            <title>The Three Tools, Side by Side</title>
            <p>
                Each of the course's three debugging tools sees something the
                others cannot:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li><term>Diagnostic <c>printf</c></term>: works at the level
                    of your C code, needs only the UART, and leaves a written
                    trail.  Its blind spot: it cannot debug the path that makes
                    <c>printf</c> itself work, and each call costs tens of
                    milliseconds at the course's 9600 baud: a 20-character
                    line is about 21 ms.</li>
                <li><term>The interactive debugger</term>: sees every register
                    and variable, needs no working UART, and steps cause to
                    effect one line at a time.  Its blind spot: a halted chip is
                    not running in real time, so time-dependent behavior changes
                    the moment you pause it.</li>
                <li><term>The DMM and AD2</term>: see the actual volts, which
                    no amount of software can misreport, and they keep watching
                    while the program runs at full speed.  Their blind spot:
                    they show what the hardware is doing, not what the program
                    intended, which is what the other two tools are for.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                A common full workflow: the scope confirms the hardware is
                doing what it should; <c>printf</c> narrows which section of
                code is at fault; the debugger shows the exact register or
                variable whose value differs from what you expected.
            </p>
        </subsection>
    </section>

</chapter>
